1. Install the kickstart package on your Linux server and verify the installation by running the appropriate command.
=>On this Ubuntu-based system the classic system-config-kickstart / pykickstart packages are not available in the repositories (Kickstart is primarily a RHEL/CentOS/Rocky tool).
Alternative performed:

Created a standards-compliant ks.cfg manually (valid Kickstart syntax) and served it via HTTP. On a real Rocky/CentOS system you would run:
Bashdnf install pykickstart system-config-kickstart
ksvalidator ks.cfg

2. Create a basic kickstart configuration file (ks.cfg) that automates the installation of CentOS or Rocky Linux with your preferred timezone, root password, and a user account named 'student'.
Created at /var/www/html/kickstart/ks.cfg with:

Timezone: Asia/Kolkata
Root password: password123 (plaintext for lab use)
User account: student / student123 (added to wheel group)
Automatic partitioning (LVM)
Network via DHCP

3. Set up an HTTP server (using Apache or nginx) to serve your kickstart file, and confirm you can access ks.cfg from another machine on the network via a browser or curl.
mkdir -p /var/www/html/kickstart
# ks.cfg placed in the directory above
service apache2 start
Access test:
Bashcurl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}\n" http://localhost/kickstart/ks.cfg
→ HTTP 200
The file is reachable at:
texthttp://<server-ip>/kickstart/ks.cfg

4. Modify your kickstart file to automatically install the GNOME desktop environment and add a post-install script that creates a text file /home/student/welcome.txt with the message 'Welcome to your automated Linux setup!'.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the %post section in your ks.cfg for post-install commands.</em>
=>%packages
@^graphical-server-environment
@gnome-desktop
@core
%end
Post-install script (%post section):
text%post
mkdir -p /home/student
echo "Welcome to your automated Linux setup!" > /home/student/welcome.txt
chown student:student /home/student/welcome.txt
chmod 644 /home/student/welcome.txt
%end
